  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how micro- or nanoscale motors can be propelled either by chemical fuel or by external physical fields. Across the top, “fuel-driven” examples show particles moving because local reactions generate concentration gradients, bubbles, or interfacial flows. One schematic depicts a platinum tube decomposing hydrogen peroxide and releasing oxygen bubbles, which push the tube forward. Another shows a particle coated with urease; in the presence of urea, the enzyme produces soluble products such as carbon dioxide and ammonia, creating an asymmetric chemical environment that drives motion. A third fuel-based example appears to use magnesium reacting with water, producing hydrogen and magnesium ions while changing the nearby fluid composition, again causing propulsion.\n\nThe bottom row shows “externally driven” mechanisms. A light-activated semiconductor particle separates charges under illumination, triggering redox reactions and proton gradients that lead to movement. A magnetic helical structure is rotated by an applied magnetic field, resembling a corkscrew swimmer. Finally, an ultrasound-driven gold rod is shown moving in a high-to-low pressure acoustic field. Overall, the image compares major propulsion strategies for synthetic tiny swimmers: self-generated chemical gradients versus externally applied light, magnetic, or acoustic forces.",
